'''vent<sup>3</sup>''' (vent), ''n.'' [< OF. ''vente'', F. ''vente'', sale, place of sale, market, = Sp. ''venta'', a sale, a market, also an inn (''hacer venta'', put up at an inn), = Pg. ''venda'' = It. ''vendita'', a sale, < ML. ''vendita'', a sale, < L. ''vendere'', pp. ''venditus'', sell: see ''vend''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''vent''<sup>4</sup>.] '''1.''' The act of selling; sale. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|An order was taken that from henceforth no printer shall print or put to ''vent'' any English book but such as shall first be examined by Mr. Secretary Petre, Mr. Secretary Smith, and Mr. Cecil, or the one of them, and allowed by the same. . . . 13th August, 1549.
{{right|''MS. Privy Council Book'', quoted in R. W. Dixon’s Hist. [Church of Eng., xvi., note.}}
The ''vent'' of ten millions of pounds of this commodity, now locked up by the operation of an injudicious tax, and rotting in the warehouses of the company, would have prevented all this distress. {{right|''Burke'', Amer. Taxation.}}}}
'''2.''' Opportunity to sell; market.
{{smaller block|We be vncertaine what ''vent'' or sale you shall finde in Persia. {{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 342.}}
Pepper . . . grows here very well, and might be had in great plenty, if it had any ''vent''.
{{right|''R. Knox'' (Arber’s Eng. Garner, I. 336).}}
There is in a manner no ''vent'' for any commodity except wool. {{right|''Sir W. Temple'', Miscellanies, p. 11.}}}}

'''vent<sup>3</sup>†''' (vent), ''v. t.'' [< ''vent''<sup>3</sup>, ''n.'' Cf. ''vend''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] To vend; sell.
{{smaller block|Whereas other English Marchants in one small Towne of Germania ''vent'' 60 or 80 thowsand clothes yearlie.
{{right|''G. Fletcher'', quoted in Ellis’s Lit. Letters, p. 83.}}
<poem>Familiar with the prices
Of oil and corn, with when and where to ''vent'' them.</poem>
{{right|''Massinger'', Great Duke of Florence, ii. 2.}}}}

'''vent<sup>4</sup>†''' (vent), ''n.'' [< Sp. ''venta'', an inn, prop. a market or place of sale: see ''vent''<sup>3</sup>.] An inn.

