'''venter'''<sup>2</sup> (ven′tėr), ''n.'' [In def. 1 < OF. ''ventre'', F. ''ventre'' = It. ''ventre''; in defs. 2 and 3 directly < L. ''venter'', the belly, womb.] '''1.''' The womb; and hence, in legal language, mother: as, A has a son B by one ''venter'', and a daughter C by another ''venter''; children by different ''venters''.—'''2.''' In ''anat.'' and ''zoöl.'', the belly; the abdomen. {{smaller|Hence—(''a'') The whole ventral aspect or surface of the body, opposite the back: opposed to ''dorsum''. (''b''†) One of the three large, as if bellying, cavities of the body containing viscera: as, the ''venter'' of the head, of the thorax, and of the abdomen: collectively called ''the three venters''. (''c'') Some swelling or protuberant part; specifically, the fleshy belly of a muscle. See ''biventer'', ''digastric'', ''n.'' (''d'') The belly or concavity of a bone, as opposed to its ''dorsum'' or convexity. [Little used, except in two of the phrases below.]}} '''3.''' In ''ornith.'', the lower belly or abdomen, considered as to its surface.
{{smaller block|Abdomen . . . has been unnecessarily divided into epigastrium or pit of the stomach, and ''venter'' or lower belly; but these terms are rarely used.
{{right|''Coues'', Key to N. A. Birds, p. 961.}}}}
'''4.''' In ''entom.'': (''a'') The lower part of the abdomen. (''b'') The under surface: as, the ''venter'' of the caterpillar.—'''5.''' In ''bot.'', the enlarged basal part of an archegonium, in which the oöphore is formed.—'''In ventre sa mere.''' {{smaller|See ''in ventre''.}}—'''Venter of the ilium,''' {{smaller|the iliac fossa.}}—'''Venter of the scapula,''' {{smaller|the scapular fossa.}}—'''Venter propendens,''' {{smaller|anteversion of the uterus.}}—'''Venter renum,''' {{smaller|the pelvis of the kidney.}}

'''vent-faucet''' (vent′fâ″set), ''n.'' A hollow gimlet or boring-instrument used to make a vent-hole in a cask or other wooden vessel, and to

